A foreign key is a column in one table that references the primary key of another table, creating a relationship between the two tables.

Why are foreign keys useful?

Without a foreign key, you could accidentally store invalid data:

id	title	ownerId
1	University	999

But if there is no user with id = 999, the board would point to someone who doesn't exist.

A foreign key prevents this by enforcing referential integrity. The database won't let you create a board with an ownerId that doesn't exist in the User table.


List Table - list is one column on a Trello board

model List {
  id        Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  title     String
  position  Int

  boardId   Int
  board     Board @relation(fields: [boardId], references: [id])

  cards     Card[]
}

position - shows order of the lists
boardId - means the list belongs to specific boardId
cards Card[] - every list contains cards


Card - card represents one task

model Card {
  id          Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  title       String
  description String?
  position    Int

  listId      Int
  list         List @relation(fields: [listId], references: [id])
}

position - shows order of the cards
listId - knows what card the list belongs to 